      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Correction for soft tissue signal attenuation can improve the diagnostic accuracy of single-photon emission computed tomography myocardial perfusion imaging (SPECT-MPI). The aim of this study was to correlate SPECT-MPI findings with clinical outcomes in patients who underwent stress imaging in the supine position, who also underwent \"second look\" stress imaging in the prone position."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Patients without perfusion abnormalities were considered Normal (N = 270). Those with apparent supine stress perfusion abnormalities which all resolved during prone imaging formed the Normal-Prone group (N = 309). Patients with matched perfusion abnormalities during both supine and prone stress imaging were considered Abnormal (N = 169)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "During follow-up (187 ± 96 days), utilization rates for invasive coronary angiography were similar for Normal vs Normal-Prone patients (3.5% vs 3.8%; P = NS), but were significantly higher in Abnormal patients (42.4%, P < .0001). Coronary revascularization occurred in 0.78%, 0.64%, and 17.7% of Normal, Normal-Prone, and Abnormal patients, respectively (P < .001). Cardiac death or myocardial infarction occurred in 2.2%, 2.3%, and 6.3% of Normal, Normal-Prone, and Abnormal patients, respectively (P = .02)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Second look SPECT-MPI identifies patients at low risk for death or myocardial infarction, who infrequently require invasive coronary angiography."
        }
      ]},
